  "body": {
    "id": "lem:factorized-pushforward-fidelity",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(G\\) and finite \\(\\mathcal J\\), \\(\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{raw}}=\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac-q}}\\subseteq\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{simp-q}}\\). More precisely, for every \\(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_G\\), push-forward gives \\(\\mathbb E_{\\mu_{\\lambda}}[T_{G,\\mathcal J}]=\\mathbb E_{(\\pi_{G,\\mathcal J})_{\\#}\\mu_{\\lambda}}[\\overline T_{G,\\mathcal J}]\\), and every member of \\(\\overline{\\mathcal P}_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac}}\\) arises this way. Thus only replacement of the induced factorized quotient family by the unrestricted branchwise quotient simplex can enlarge the projected image.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "G",
      "\\mathcal J",
      "\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{raw}}",
      "\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac-q}}",
      "\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{simp-q}}",
      "\\lambda",
      "\\Lambda_G",
      "\\mu_{\\lambda}",
      "T_{G,\\mathcal J}",
      "\\pi_{G,\\mathcal J}",
      "\\overline T_{G,\\mathcal J}",
      "\\overline{\\mathcal P}_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac}}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:independent-edge-latents",
      "def:raw-global-signatures",
      "def:response-signature-quotient",
      "def:factorized-quotient-images"
    ],
    "justification": "This isolates the only dangerous step: push-forward itself is exact, whereas freeing quotient-cell masses can erase the nonlinear latent-factor constraints.",
    "gap": "The unrestricted-simplex lifts in RossettoAntonucci2026 and ChoeEtAl2026 establish linear-program fidelity but do not establish membership in the induced independent-edge-latent quotient family.",
    "consumer": "The quotient search can test the unrestricted reduced program against a precisely defined sound factorized benchmark.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_G\\) and put \\(\\mu=\\mu_\\lambda\\).  For every coordinate \\(c\\) of the vector \\(T_{G,\\mathcal J}\\), the defining identity \\(\\overline T_{G,\\mathcal J}\\circ\\pi_{G,\\mathcal J}=T_{G,\\mathcal J}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{(\\pi_{G,\\mathcal J})_\\#\\mu}[\\overline T_{G,\\mathcal J,c}]\n&=\\sum_{C\\in\\overline\\Omega_{G,\\mathcal J}}\\left(\\sum_{\\omega\\in C}\\mu(\\omega)\\right)\\overline T_{G,\\mathcal J,c}(C)\\\\\n&=\\sum_C\\sum_{\\omega\\in C}\\mu(\\omega)T_{G,\\mathcal J,c}(\\omega)\\\\\n&=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega_{G,\\mathcal J}}\\mu(\\omega)T_{G,\\mathcal J,c}(\\omega)\\\\\n&=\\mathbb E_\\mu[T_{G,\\mathcal J,c}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nThe quotient cells partition the finite raw domain, so every rearrangement in (26) is exact.  By the definition of \\(\\overline{\\mathcal P}_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac}}\\), every one of its members is \\((\\pi_{G,\\mathcal J})_\\#\\mu_\\lambda\\) for some \\(\\lambda\\), and every such push-forward is a member.  Taking vector expectations in (26) proves\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{raw}}\n=\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{fac-q}}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\mu_\\lambda\\) is supported on the single branch selected by \\(\\lambda\\), its push-forward is a probability mass on that branch's quotient cells.  It is therefore feasible for the unrestricted branchwise simplex in \\(\\mathcal A_{G,\\mathcal J}^{\\mathrm{simp-q}}\\), which proves the inclusion.  No reverse inclusion was used: it is exactly the independent-factor restriction on quotient masses that the larger simplex discards.",
